From: nix-wasm <eric@codebutler.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: boot-time wall clock via read_persistent_clock64

The wasm arch registered only a clocksource + clockevent; with no
read_persistent_clock64 the weak default in kernel/time/timekeeping.c
returns 0 and CLOCK_REALTIME boots at the 1970 epoch. That surfaced the
moment the guest did real TLS (Cachix substitution over the wan0 uplink):
curl/openssl reject every certificate as "not yet valid" (curl error 60),
so nix treats the cache as empty and falls back to a doomed from-source
plan — the same visible failure shape as the missing-CA-bundle bug it hid
behind.

Fix: implement read_persistent_clock64 from the engine's EXISTING
wasm_cpu_clock_get_monotonic import. kernel-worker.js anchors that value at
the UNIX epoch (performance.timeOrigin + performance.now(), in ns), so its
absolute value IS real wall time; the timekeeping core keeps consuming only
deltas of it through the clocksource, and the one absolute read at boot
initializes the wall clock. The epoch anchoring becomes load-bearing for
this function — an engine returning a 0-based monotonic value degrades
back to the 1970 boot (no crash) — and the wire surface is unchanged, so
no ENGINE_ABI bump and no pc engine sync (pc's vendored kernel-worker.js
uses the identical timeOrigin formula).

Gated by runtime/demo/node/clock-smoke.mjs (busybox-only boot: `date +%s`
must be within tolerance of the host clock).

--- a/arch/wasm/kernel/time.c	2026-07-22 02:30:58.600105479 +0000
+++ b/arch/wasm/kernel/time.c	2026-07-22 02:32:08.561980278 +0000
@@ -3,6 +3,8 @@
 #include <linux/clocksource.h>
 #include <linux/clockchips.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/time64.h>
+#include <linux/timekeeping.h>
 
 #include <asm/irq.h>
 #include <asm/smp.h>
@@ -10,6 +12,27 @@
 
 extern unsigned long long wasm_cpu_clock_get_monotonic(void);
 
+/*
+ * Boot-time wall clock (the "RTC"). Without this override the weak default
+ * in kernel/time/timekeeping.c returns 0 and CLOCK_REALTIME starts at the
+ * 1970 epoch — every TLS certificate is then "not yet valid", which broke
+ * the guest's HTTPS substitution from nix-wasm.cachix.org.
+ *
+ * The engine's wasm_cpu_clock_get_monotonic import is anchored at the UNIX
+ * epoch (kernel-worker.js returns performance.timeOrigin + performance.now()
+ * in ns), so the very same import doubles as the persistent clock: its
+ * absolute value is real wall time, while the timekeeping core continues to
+ * consume only deltas of it through the clocksource. That epoch anchoring is
+ * LOAD-BEARING for this function — an engine returning a 0-based monotonic
+ * value degrades back to the 1970 boot (no crash), so this needs no
+ * ENGINE_ABI bump: the wire surface is unchanged and both shipped engines
+ * (nix-wasm runtime/ + pc's vendored copy) already anchor at timeOrigin.
+ */
+void read_persistent_clock64(struct timespec64 *ts)
+{
+	*ts = ns_to_timespec64(wasm_cpu_clock_get_monotonic());
+}
+
 /* Wasm clock source: derived from Wasm host cpu clock (monotonic). */
 
 static unsigned long long wasm_clocksource_read(struct clocksource *cs)
